      en: "The Gulf of California, also known as the Sea of Cortez, is one of the most productive and diverse marine ecosystems on Earth. Famed explorer Jacques Cousteau famously dubbed it the 'World's Aquarium' due to its extraordinary abundance of marine life. This narrow body of water, separating the Baja California Peninsula from the Mexican mainland, is a UNESCO World Heritage site and home to nearly 40% of the world's total marine mammal species. It is a critical breeding and nursery ground for whales, including the blue, fin, and humpback. The Gulf is also the only place on Earth where the critically endangered vaquita porpoise is found. Its unique combination of deep basins, shallow shelves, and nutrient-rich upwellings supports a vibrant food web that sustains both extraordinary biodiversity and vital regional fisheries."
